canonicalUrl https://paulgraham.com/fix.html

post-header.scroll

Kevin Kelleher suggested an interesting way to compare programming languages: to describe each in terms of the problem it fixes. The surprising thing is how many, and how well, languages can be described this way.

Algol: Assembly language is too low-level.
 bold Algol:

Pascal: Algol doesn't have enough data types.
 bold Pascal:

Modula: Pascal is too wimpy for systems programming.
 bold Modula:

Simula: Algol isn't good enough at simulations.
 bold Simula:

Smalltalk: Not everything in Simula is an object.
 bold Smalltalk:

Fortran: Assembly language is too low-level.
 bold Fortran:

Cobol: Fortran is scary.
 bold Cobol:

PL/1: Fortran doesn't have enough data types.
 bold PL/1:

Ada: Every existing language is missing something.
 bold Ada:

Basic: Fortran is scary.
 bold Basic:

APL: Fortran isn't good enough at manipulating arrays.
 bold APL:

J: APL requires its own character set.
 bold J:

C: Assembly language is too low-level.
 bold C:

C++: C is too low-level.
 bold C++:

Java: C++ is a kludge. And Microsoft is going to crush us.
 bold Java:

C#: Java is controlled by Sun.
 bold C#:

Lisp: Turing Machines are an awkward way to describe computation.
 bold Lisp:

Scheme: MacLisp is a kludge.
 bold Scheme:

T: Scheme has no libraries.
 bold T:

Common Lisp: There are too many dialects of Lisp.
 bold Common Lisp:

Dylan: Scheme has no libraries, and Lisp syntax is scary.
 bold Dylan:

Perl: Shell scripts/awk/sed are not enough like programming languages.
 bold Perl:

Python: Perl is a kludge.
 bold Python:

Ruby: Perl is a kludge, and Lisp syntax is scary.
 bold Ruby:

Prolog: Programming is not enough like logic.
 bold Prolog:

